  "preview": "A rectangular pool sits inside a rectangular deck. The deck is the same width on every side. Write an expression for the area of just the deck — then figure out how wide the deck should be if the homeowner has 200 sq ft of decking to spend.",
  "body": {
    "goal": "Write an expression for the area of a frame-shaped region by subtraction (outer rectangle minus inner rectangle), simplify using the distributive property, and use the expression to solve a real-world design problem.",
    "studentPrompt": "A homeowner is installing a rectangular swimming pool with a wooden deck around it. The pool is 18 feet long and 10 feet wide. The deck has the same width w on every side, so it forms a frame around the pool.\n\n1. Write an expression (in terms of w) for the area of ONLY the deck — not the pool. Simplify it.\n2. If the deck is 3 feet wide on every side, what is the area of the deck?\n3. The homeowner can afford 200 square feet of decking. How wide should w be? Give your answer to the nearest tenth of a foot.\n4. The homeowner's neighbor has the same pool but wants a deck on only THREE sides (the back side is against the house). Write a new expression for that deck's area. How does it compare to your Q1 expression?",
    "image": {
      "kind": "url",
      "url": "https://images.pexels.com/photos/261106/pexels-photo-261106.jpeg?auto=compress&cs=tinysrgb&h=650&w=940",
      "alt": "A straw hat rests on a wooden deck beside a sparkling blue swimming pool, perfect for summer relaxation.",
      "credit": "Photo by Pixabay on Pexels"
    },
    "launch": "Show the picture. Read the setup. 'The deck is the frame around the pool. Find an expression for just the deck area.' Random groups, vertical whiteboards. If a group gets stuck, ask them to find the area of the whole thing (deck + pool) and the area of just the pool — and notice the relationship.",
    "materials": [
      "Vertical whiteboards",
      "Markers",
      "Calculator allowed for Q3"
    ],
    "concepts": [
      "area",
      "composite-figures",
      "expressions",
      "distributive-property",
      "subtraction-of-areas",
      "equations"
    ],
    "taskType": "investigation",
    "btcFit": "Solid BTC task. Low floor (sketch and compute). Q1 is the diamond — students must reason about subtracting areas, and many will need to be reminded that the outer rectangle dimensions are (18 + 2w) and (10 + 2w), not (18 + w) and (10 + w). Q3 forces a quadratic-ish equation that they can solve by estimation. Q4 invites a structural comparison.",
    "anticipatedApproaches": "- Q1: Outer rectangle = (18 + 2w)(10 + 2w). Pool = 18 × 10 = 180. Deck area = (18 + 2w)(10 + 2w) - 180. Expanding: 180 + 36w + 20w + 4w^2 - 180 = 4w^2 + 56w. Some groups will decompose the frame into four rectangles (two long strips of 18 × w on top and bottom, two short strips of 10 × w on the sides) plus four w × w corner squares: 2(18w) + 2(10w) + 4w^2 = 36w + 20w + 4w^2 = 4w^2 + 56w. Same answer, different decomposition.\n- Q2: w = 3. Deck area = 4(9) + 56(3) = 36 + 168 = 204 sq ft. (Or: outer = 24 × 16 = 384, minus pool 180 = 204.)\n- Q3: Set 4w^2 + 56w = 200. Divide by 4: w^2 + 14w = 50. By estimation, w ≈ 2.95. (At w = 3, area = 204. At w = 2.9, area = 4(8.41) + 56(2.9) = 33.64 + 162.4 = 196.04. So between 2.9 and 3 — about 2.95.) Accept any answer between 2.9 and 3.0.\n- Q4: Deck on three sides (no deck on the back along the 18-ft side). One long strip of 18 × w plus two short strips of (10 + w) × w on the sides. Area = 18w + 2w(10 + w) = 18w + 20w + 2w^2 = 2w^2 + 38w. About half of Q1's expression — but not exactly half, because the corner squares aren't symmetric.",
    "commonMisconceptions": "- Using (18 + w)(10 + w) for the outer rectangle — forgetting that the deck adds w on BOTH sides.\n- Forgetting to subtract the pool, and just reporting the outer rectangle area.\n- For Q1, distributing wrong: (18 + 2w)(10 + 2w) = 180 + 2w · 2w = 180 + 4w^2 (forgetting the cross terms).\n- For Q3, solving 4w^2 + 56w = 200 by ignoring the w^2 term: 56w = 200 → w ≈ 3.57 (wrong).\n- For Q4, forgetting that without a back side, the two side strips run the full (10 + w) length, not just 10.",
    "discussionQuestions": "- Two groups got 4w^2 + 56w. One subtracted (outer - pool). The other added four strips and four corners. Show how those two methods agree term-by-term.\n- For Q3, the answer wasn't a whole number. How did you decide between 2.9 and 3.0?\n- In Q4, the three-sided deck isn't exactly half of the four-sided deck. Why not?",
    "extensions": "- The homeowner finds 250 square feet of deck on clearance. How wide can w be now?\n- Suppose the pool's dimensions change to L feet by W feet, with a uniform deck of width w. Write a general expression for the deck's area in terms of L, W, and w.\n- The deck costs $8 per square foot of wood, plus $40 per linear foot for a railing around the outer edge. If the homeowner has $2,000 total, can they afford a 3-foot-wide deck?",
    "sampleSolutions": "1. Outer rectangle: (18 + 2w)(10 + 2w) = 180 + 36w + 20w + 4w^2 = 4w^2 + 56w + 180. Subtract pool (180): Deck area = 4w^2 + 56w.\n2. w = 3: Deck area = 4(9) + 56(3) = 36 + 168 = 204 sq ft.\n3. Solve 4w^2 + 56w = 200, or w^2 + 14w - 50 = 0. Quadratic formula: w = (-14 + √(196 + 200))/2 = (-14 + √396)/2 ≈ (-14 + 19.9)/2 ≈ 2.95 ft. (Accept w ≈ 2.9 or 3.0 ft.)\n4. Three-sided deck (no deck along one long side): 18w (one long strip) + 2 · w(10 + w) (two side strips that run past where the back deck would have been) = 18w + 20w + 2w^2 = 2w^2 + 38w. At w = 3: 18 + 114 = 132 sq ft. Compared to 204 for the four-sided deck — less than half, because removing the back strip also removes two corner squares.",
